# Post-mortem -- slice 0252, attempt 02

## Summary

The normal profile-1 resume of 0252 ran green end to end: no product edit, review-green, and all 19 gates passed under the supervisor. It then escalated because the verify phase's tracked-tree fingerprint changed while no tracked content changed.
The fingerprint hashes git's stderr. The boot had re-emitted 486 descriptors with CRLF line endings, so git's stderr carried 486 CRLF warnings. An index refresh at 14:08:05 UTC by a process outside the gate roster silenced them, and the hash moved: a false positive in the tool.

## Root cause

Owner: **the tool** (the runner's tracked-tree fingerprint). Before the verify gates run, `run_phases.py:688-713` takes `tracked_tree_fingerprint`. It takes it again after them and escalates on any difference with "a verification gate unexpectedly changed tracked source".

`tracked_tree_fingerprint` (`pipeline/state.py:103-105`) and `tree_fingerprint` (`96-100`) both hash `tracked_diff_bytes` (`82-93`). That function returns `proc.stdout + proc.stderr` of `git diff --binary HEAD`. Its stderr carries git's safecrlf warnings ("in the working copy of X, CRLF will be replaced by LF…"). Git emits one only for a CRLF file whose index stat entry is stale, that is, one it has to re-read. So the hash depends on the git index's stat cache, not on tracked content. `cleaned_sha256` normalizes line endings but cannot remove whole lines.

The step-compiler supplies the precondition at every boot. It re-emitted all 243 non-completed `.slice-md` and `.gates` files (13:58:42–43 UTC) through `step_compiler/compile.py:1706`, `write_text(..., encoding="utf-8")` with no `newline="\n"`. On Windows that writes CRLF against the repository's `* text=auto eol=lf`, and `git ls-files --eol` shows `i/lf w/crlf`. That leaves 486 content-identical files with fresh mtimes, each producing a warning.

At review time the diff's stderr held 488 warnings: the 486 descriptors, `.executability-json` and `.supervisor-lock.diag`. `.git/index` was then rewritten at 14:08:05 UTC, inside the elevation gate's run. After that refresh only the last two warnings remain. Those two persist because their CRLF size differs from the size the index recorded, so a refresh cannot clear them.

I reproduced both recorded fingerprints byte-exactly with ASG's own `cleaned_sha256`. The current stdout plus the current 2 warnings gives `70df52014dd4…`, the state's value after the escalation. The same stdout plus the review-time 488 warnings gives `90f20879e95d…`, the REVIEW-ATTESTED and verify PHASE-START value. The diff text is identical, and no tracked file except the two excluded supervisor files has an mtime inside the verify window.

I could not identify what wrote the index at 14:08:05. It is outside the gate roster:
- The elevation script's git calls use `--no-optional-locks` and run at its start (14:05:33).
- The runner's own repeated `git diff` calls did not clear the warnings between 14:03:04 and 14:05:31.
- 0210's resume ran the same roster over the same precondition (572 warnings) and passed.

The most likely writer is an interactive git client or IDE on the repository (`.git/` shows a second lock touch at 14:08:35), but this is unverified. It does not change the owner: a check that a gate changed tracked source must not move when something refreshes the index.

This is the second false-mutation variant of the same check. ASG 0090 (033) (`ASG-ERRORS.md` row 65) fixed the `.manual/`/`.specrc` variant by path exclusion. Path exclusion cannot reach a diagnostic about a path that is still in scope, which is why that fix missed this case. The class fix is to hash content only.

ASG commit `d93561be` (13:56 UTC, two minutes before this boot) is the only tool change since the last green slice. It threads a budget-warning sink into `evaluate_all_gates` and writes only the git-ignored `.artifacts/operator-warnings.json`, which is absent. It is cleared as a suspect; the fingerprint code last changed on 2026-11-08.

